   <front>
     <title>Proposed Standard Socket Numbers</title>
     <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="349" stream="IETF"/>
-    <date year="1972" month="May"/>
+    <author fullname="Jon Postel" initials="J." surname="Postel">
+      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">UCLA-NMC</organization>
+      <address/>
+    </author>
+    <date year="1972" month="May" day="30"/>
     <abstract pn="section-abstract">
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">I propose that there be a czar (me ?) who hands out official socket
+numbers for use by standard protocols.  This czar should also keep track
+of and publish a list of those socket numbers where host specific
+services can be obtained.  I further suggest that the initial allocation
+be as follows:</t>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-abstract-2"><![CDATA[
+Sockets         Assignment
+0-63            Network wide standard functions
+64-127          Host specific functions
+128-239         Reserved for future use
+240-255         Any experimental function
+]]></artwork>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-3">and within the network wide standard functions the following particular
+assignment be made:</t>
+      <artwork name="" type="" align="left" alt="" pn="section-abstract-4"><![CDATA[
+Socket          Assignment
+   1            Telnet
+   3            File Transfer
+   5            Remote Job Entry
+   7            Echo
+   9            Discard
+]]></artwork>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-5">these socket numbers (decimal) are to be used for the socket called "L"
+in the official Initial Connection protocol (ICP) as specified in NIC
+7104 the "Current Network Protocols" notebook.</t>
+      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-6">[ This RFC was put into machine readable form for entry ]
+[ into the online RFC archives by BBN Corp. under the   ]
+[ direction of Alex McKenzie.                   12/96   ]</t>
     </abstract>
